1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,034
Lesson 9.1.

2
00:00:02,414 --> 00:00:03,743
Diffusion models.

3
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:09,082
Diffusion models generate images,
audio and video by destroying data

4
00:00:09,082 --> 00:00:12,941
with noise little by little and
learning to undo that process.

5
00:00:13,321 --> 00:00:17,947
Deep down, they are Langevin
dynamics driven by a neural network.

6
00:00:18,327 --> 00:00:21,362
That is why they fit thermodynamic
computing so well: the

7
00:00:21,362 --> 00:00:24,184
hardware already knows how
to do stochastic dynamics.

8
00:00:24,564 --> 00:00:26,931
Today we see the theory
and measure how much

9
00:00:26,931 --> 00:00:29,245
the sampler and the
number of steps matter.

10
00:00:30,350 --> 00:00:38,548
The forward process is an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck
process: dx = −x dt + √2 dW.

11
00:00:38,928 --> 00:00:43,808
Starting from a data
point x₀, at time t the

12
00:00:43,808 --> 00:00:48,134
sample is e^(−t) x₀ + √(1 − e^(−2t)) ε.

13
00:00:48,514 --> 00:00:53,118
Given enough time, all structure is
erased and a standard Gaussian remains.

14
00:00:53,498 --> 00:00:58,488
In our example, two modes at plus and
minus two merge into a single bell.

15
00:00:59,617 --> 00:01:05,098
Anderson showed in nineteen eighty-two that
the process has a time reversal, another

16
00:01:05,098 --> 00:01:09,862
stochastic equation whose drift includes
the gradient of the log-density,

17
00:01:10,242 --> 00:01:13,418
∇ log p_t, called the score.

18
00:01:13,798 --> 00:01:16,862
If we know the score at
every time, we can start

19
00:01:16,862 --> 00:01:19,990
from noise and run time
backwards to obtain data.

20
00:01:20,370 --> 00:01:23,143
All the learning consists
of estimating that field.

21
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:29,669
DDPM trains a network that predicts the
noise ε added to a noisy data point.

22
00:01:30,049 --> 00:01:34,318
It turns out that predicting
the noise is equivalent

23
00:01:34,318 --> 00:01:38,013
to estimating the score:
∇ log p_t ≈ −ε̂/σ_t.

24
00:01:38,393 --> 00:01:41,465
That is denoising score matching.

25
00:01:41,845 --> 00:01:45,539
The loss is a simple squared
error, stable and easy to

26
00:01:45,539 --> 00:01:48,754
scale, one of the reasons
these models succeed.

27
00:01:49,883 --> 00:01:51,351
There are two ways to sample.

28
00:01:51,732 --> 00:01:55,211
The reverse stochastic equation
adds noise at every step.

29
00:01:55,591 --> 00:01:59,053
The probability-flow ordinary
differential equation

30
00:01:59,053 --> 00:02:02,040
is deterministic and
has the same marginals.

31
00:02:02,420 --> 00:02:05,955
In our example, with the exact
score of a mixture of two

32
00:02:05,955 --> 00:02:09,995
Gaussians, we measure the Wasserstein
distance to the true data.

33
00:02:10,375 --> 00:02:14,235
With five steps both fail, near 0.87.

34
00:02:14,615 --> 00:02:20,305
With fifty, the stochastic one reaches
0.16 and the ordinary one 0.11.

35
00:02:20,685 --> 00:02:24,374
With two hundred, 0.07 and 0.03.

36
00:02:25,483 --> 00:02:29,359
Each step costs one network
evaluation: on a GPU, the cost of

37
00:02:29,359 --> 00:02:32,599
generating grows linearly
with the number of steps.

38
00:02:32,979 --> 00:02:35,953
That is why so much
research seeks few-step

39
00:02:35,953 --> 00:02:39,342
samplers, better time
schedules and distillation.

40
00:02:39,722 --> 00:02:44,074
For thermodynamic hardware the
question changes: if each step is

41
00:02:44,074 --> 00:02:47,745
cheap physical dynamics, how
many steps can we afford?

42
00:02:48,125 --> 00:02:50,651
That is the idea that the denoising

43
00:02:50,651 --> 00:02:54,114
thermodynamic models of
lesson 9.3 will develop.

44
00:02:55,217 --> 00:03:00,258
Diffusion models dominate the generation
of images, video, audio and molecules.

45
00:03:00,638 --> 00:03:05,560
In practice they are combined with
samplers such as DDIM and DPM-Solver,

46
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:09,730
careful schedules, distillation
and classifier-free guidance.

47
00:03:10,110 --> 00:03:13,620
In lab twelve you will train a small DDPM.

48
00:03:14,750 --> 00:03:18,048
Common mistakes: confusing
the forward process, which

49
00:03:18,048 --> 00:03:20,848
is fixed, with the
reverse, which is learned.

50
00:03:21,228 --> 00:03:23,931
Forgetting the factor
relating noise and score.

51
00:03:24,311 --> 00:03:28,992
Using a uniform time grid, when the
error concentrates near the end.

52
00:03:29,372 --> 00:03:32,537
And comparing samplers with
different numbers of evaluations.

53
00:03:33,650 --> 00:03:37,066
In summary: a diffusion
model destroys data with an

54
00:03:37,066 --> 00:03:41,218
Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process and
learns the score to reverse it.

55
00:03:41,599 --> 00:03:43,813
Predicting the noise is
estimating the score.

56
00:03:44,193 --> 00:03:47,667
One samples with the reverse
stochastic equation or the

57
00:03:47,667 --> 00:03:51,393
probability-flow ordinary one,
and quality depends strongly

58
00:03:51,393 --> 00:03:54,614
on the number of steps,
which on a GPU is the cost.

59
00:03:55,717 --> 00:03:59,451
To practice: write the marginal
of the forward process.

60
00:03:59,831 --> 00:04:02,346
Prove the relation
between noise and score.

61
00:04:02,726 --> 00:04:05,915
Write the reverse equation
and the probability-flow one.

62
00:04:06,295 --> 00:04:09,375
And explain why a uniform
grid wastes steps.
